            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <h3 id="" class="zn-heading "   >TASTE JOHNNY APPLESEED'S HANDIWORK</h3>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-872129569991"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p><a href="https://altapassorchard.org" target="_blank">The Orchard at Altapass</a> in Spruce Pine, North Carolina (milepost 328.3), is less of an orchard and more of an apple museum. Stroll the 277-acre property, and you’ll find heirloom cultivars dating back hundreds of years. </p><p><br></p><p>Varieties range from the Crow’s Egg, a squatty little snack discovered in the 1700s, to the Grimes Golden, a sweet and tangy fruit supposedly first planted by Johnny Appleseed. There’s also the nobly-named King Luscious, a Depression-era apple quite distinguished in taste.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

